ber.isFinite(cap) || cap < 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(`capacity for desk ${desk} must be a finite non-negative number`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but I should also think about whether capacityByDesk itself needs all its values to be valid, or only the ones referenced by requests. The problem says: \"capacityByDesk: an object containing a finite, non-negative capacity for every desk referenced by a request.\" So only the referenced desks need valid capacities. Other keys in capacityByDesk are not our concern. Good, my validation only checks referenced desks.\n\nNow `toTimestamp`:\n\n```js\nfunction toTimestamp(submittedAt) {\n  if (submittedAt instanceof Date) {\n    return submittedAt.getTime();\n  }\n  const d = new Date(submittedAt);\n  return d.getTime();\n}\n```\n\nBut `new Date(someObject)` could produce invalid date. We check NaN. Also `new Date(undefined)` is invalid date (NaN). Good.\n\nHmm, but what about `submittedAt` being a number? `new Date(123456789)` is valid. That's fine.\n\nNow the allocation:\n\n```js\nexport function allocateCapacity(requests, capacityByDesk) {\n  validateInputs(requests, capacityByDesk);\n\n  // Build remaining capacity per desk without mutating the input.\n  const remaining = new Map();\n  for (const request of requests) {\n    if (!remaining.has(request.desk)) {\n      remaining.set(request.desk, capacityByDesk[request.desk]);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Sort indices by: priority desc, submittedAt asc, id asc.\n  const order = requests.map((_, i) => i);\n  order.sort((i, j) => {\n    const a = requests[i];\n    const b = requests[j];\n    if (a.priority !== b.priority) return b.priority - a.priority;\n    const ta = toTimestamp(a.submittedAt);\n    const tb = toTimestamp(b.submittedAt);\n    if (ta !== tb) return ta - tb;\n    if (a.id < b.id) return -1;\n    if (a.id > b.id) return 1;\n    return 0;\n  });\n\n  const allocations = new Array(requests.length);\n  for (const i of order) {\n    const request = requests[i];\n    const wanted = request.requested;\n    const avail = remaining.get(request.desk);\n    const allocated = Math.min(wanted, avail);\n    remaining.set(request.desk, avail - allocated);\n    allocations[i] = { id: request.id, allocated, unfilled: wanted - allocated };\n  }\n\n  return allocations;\n}\n```\n\nWait, there's a subtle issue. `avail` could be such that `avail - allocated` introduces floating point. But that's fine; the values are exact for integers.\n\nNow, `Math.min(wanted, avail)` — both are finite numbers, good. `allocated` could be 0 if avail is 0. unfilled = wanted - 0 = wanted. Good.\n\nThis returns rows in original input order because I index by `i` into `allocations[i]`. Good.\n\nNon-mutation: I create `order` array (new). I create `remaining` Map (new). I don't touch `requests` array or records or `capacityByDesk`. Good.\n\nBut wait — `capacityByDesk[request.desk]` — what if `desk` is a string like \"toString\" or \"__proto__\"? That's an edge case. `capacityByDesk.toString` would be the function. Hmm. Let me use `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call` or just access via bracket. Actually if desk is \"toString\" and capacityByDesk is a plain object `{ ABS: 100 }`, then `capacityByDesk.toString` is `Object.prototype.toString`, a function, not a number, so validation would catch it as invalid capacity. But that's a weird edge case. The problem says \"every desk referenced by a request\" must have a capacity. If someone passes desk \"toString\" without a toString capacity... well, `desk in capacityByDesk` would be true (inherited). Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. To be robust, I could check `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(capacityByDesk, desk)`. But the problem doesn't explicitly require own property. Let me keep it simple but use `hasOwnProperty` to be safe. Actually, let me think: if desk is \"toString\" and not in capacityByDesk as own property, should I throw? The problem says \"capacityByDesk: an object containing a finite, non-negative 